# --- wk sysimage: every image built for the rpi5 -------------------------------
# Appended to the target's local.conf by `wk sysimage build`, last, so bitbake
# takes it. What belongs here is what the *board* requires of any image built
# for it; what a measurement wants belongs to the profile.

# The Pi 5 exists in two steppings and they need different device trees. The
# 16 GB and later boards are D0, where the GPIO banks are narrower (32/4 and
# 15/6 against C0's) and the pinctrl registers are laid out differently, so the
# kernel selects the pin data by compatible: `brcm,bcm2712d0-pinctrl` against
# `brcm,bcm2712c0-pinctrl`.
#
# meta-raspberrypi at the rev these branches pin lists one Pi 5 device tree,
# broadcom/bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb, whose pinctrl nodes carry the stepping-agnostic
# `brcm,bcm2712-pinctrl` -- which that same kernel's driver maps to the *C0*
# pin data (bcm2712_c0_plat_data, drivers/pinctrl/bcm/pinctrl-bcm2712.c). On a
# D0 board the firmware asks for bcm2712d0-rpi-5-b.dtb, does not find it, falls
# back to that one, and the first pull-config write lands at a C0 offset the
# SoC aborts: an uncontainable SError, and a kernel panic in
# bcm2712_pull_config_set before rootfs (measured on the rpi5's HDMI console,
# 2026-09-04).
#
# The pinned kernel already builds the right tree -- the dts is in the rev, and
# the driver already matches d0 -- so this asks for it by name rather than
# moving the pin. Both trees ship: which one a board gets is the firmware's
# choice from the board revision, not this image's.
RPI_KERNEL_DEVICETREE:append = " broadcom/bcm2712d0-rpi-5-b.dtb"
